.TH smi-nginx-flatten "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-nginx-flatten \- Print an nginx configuration with every include expanded in place
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-nginx-flatten
.RI [ CONF_FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-nginx-flatten
prints
.IR CONF_FILE ,
.I /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
by default, with the contents of every
.B include
put where the include line was, so the whole configuration nginx actually reads can be read as
one file.
.PP
Each file that is opened is announced with a
.BI "# ----" " file"
comment line, and the lines of an included file keep the indentation the include line had, so the
nesting stays visible.
An include pattern is expanded by the shell, relative to the directory of
.IR CONF_FILE ,
so
.B include conf.d/*.conf
expands to the files that match, in the order the shell sorts them, with
.B LC_ALL=C
so that order does not depend on the machine's language.
A pattern that matches nothing, and an include of a file that does not exist, expand to nothing
and are not reported.
.PP
Nothing is changed on disk and nginx is not started;
it is a reading aid, and
.B nginx -T
remains the one that shows what the running nginx has loaded.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I CONF_FILE
Configuration to print.
Default
.IR /etc/nginx/nginx.conf .
Includes are resolved relative to its directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The configuration was printed.
.TP
.B 1
.I CONF_FILE
does not exist, or includes are nested deeper than 30 levels.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
The whole configuration in a pager:
.RS
.nf
smi-nginx-flatten | less
.fi
.RE
.PP
Where a setting really comes from:
.RS
.nf
smi-nginx-flatten | grep -n -B20 ssl_protocols | grep "# ----"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A virtual host of its own:
.RS
.nf
smi-nginx-flatten /etc/nginx/conf.d/vhosts/customer.com.conf
.fi
.RE
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
Command written by Imre Tabur.
